INT. TRAUMA BAY - NIGHT

A curtained bay under hard white light. Monitors beep. A gurney sits canted in the center, stained sheets bunched at the foot. Instruments on a steel tray, a red crash cart, a clock that reads 2:47.

DR. SADIE OYELARAN, 30s, surgical cap, sleeves pushed up, stands over the gurney with both hands on the chest of an unseen patient and counts under her breath. Across the gurney, NURSE BENEDICT "BEN" KOWALCZYK, 40s, squeezes a bag-valve mask on a rhythm. At the foot, with a clipboard, stands the resident DR. ILSE VANTERPOOL, 20s, pale, hair coming loose. Nearest the door, arms folded, watches the attending, DR. MOSES HARGROVE, 60s, silver hair, a stethoscope draped like a scarf.

SADIE
Thirty. Check.

BEN
No pulse.

SADIE
Again.

She resumes compressions. The monitor spikes, flatlines, spikes. Ilse writes a note and drops the pen.

ILSE
Dr. Hargrove, we're at twelve minutes.

HARGROVE
I can read a clock, Dr. Vanterpool.

ILSE
Sir, I think we should--

HARGROVE
Think quietly.

BEN
Sadie, your arms.

SADIE
They're fine.

BEN
Switch with me.

SADIE
I said they're fine.

He lets go of the mask, rounds the gurney, and puts a hand on her shoulder. She does not look up. He waits. After a beat she steps aside, wiping her brow with her forearm, and he takes her place. She takes the bag-valve mask.

SADIE (CONT'D)
Ben. Count.

BEN
(compressing)
One, two, three, four.

The monitor beeps once. Everyone freezes. It beeps again, uneven. Ilse looks up. Hargrove uncrosses his arms.

ILSE
That's a rhythm.

HARGROVE
That's an artifact.

SADIE
(still bagging)
That's a rhythm.

HARGROVE
Dr. Oyelaran.

SADIE
Look at the screen, Moses.

He does. A ragged line climbs and descends, climbs and descends.

BEN
(breathless)
Pulse. I've got a pulse.

SADIE
Pressure?

ILSE
Seventy over forty.

SADIE
Fluids. Wide open.

Ilse darts to the cart. Hargrove steps forward, takes the stethoscope from his neck, presses it to the patient's chest, and listens. The line on the monitor holds. He straightens slowly.

HARGROVE
Good work.

SADIE
That's not a thing you say. That's a thing you do.

HARGROVE
(quietly)
Meaning?

SADIE
Meaning you called time of death in your head six minutes ago.

HARGROVE
I did not.

SADIE
You did.

Ben's compressions slow. Ilse has stopped midway to the cart.

BEN
(low)
Sadie.

SADIE
No. He did. Say it, Moses.

HARGROVE
(after a long pause)
I did.

SADIE
Thank you.

She steps back, peels off a glove, and flexes her hand. Ben takes the mask and bags, gently. Ilse brings a bag of fluids and hangs it on the pole. The monitor beeps on, steady, small, alive.

Hargrove turns to the door. He pauses there, back to them.

HARGROVE (CONT'D)
I'll tell the family.

SADIE
No. I will.

HARGROVE
They'll ask who made the call.

SADIE
Then I'll tell them she did.

She looks at Ilse. Ilse looks at her. Neither says anything.

Hargrove exits. The curtain swings behind him. Sadie leans over the sink and runs cold water over her wrists. Ben watches her.

BEN
You okay?

SADIE
(to the water)
Ask me in an hour.

Ilse pulls the sheet up over the patient's chest and, after a moment, tucks it under the chin as if the patient were a child.
